bevy-horizon: NAK_DEBUG=lsink, fragment inputs read inside the large loop that uses them

For mesa-switch (https://github.com/danfromtico/mesa-switch, by danfromtico and NaGaa95; MIT)
at 606441c9, after 0001 to 0010. scripts/build_nvk.sh applies it; this text before the first
"diff --git" line is ignored by git apply.

0005's NAK_DEBUG=sink interpolates each fragment input where it is first used, but
nir_opt_sink never moves anything into a loop, and nir_opt_gcm, given NAK's
hoist_tex_from_loops, lifts every texture instruction out of one. So in a shader whose body is
one large loop, the inputs and texture size queries it reads stay live across the whole loop,
back edge included.

NAK_DEBUG=lsink adds nak_nir_sink_inputs_into_loops, run just after sink. A fragment input
(load_interpolated_input, load_input or load_frag_coord) whose uses all lie inside a loop of 100
instructions or more that the load is not in moves to the block that dominates them all, or to
just before a smaller loop inside that one which holds them all. 100 is GCM's own
MAX_LOOP_INSTRUCTIONS: GCM hoists such work out of smaller loops anyway. lsink also passes
hoist_tex_from_loops = false to nir_opt_gcm, so texture instructions leave only the smaller
loops, as ALU does. Each trip then interpolates or queries again, which gives the same value
wherever it runs (NAK lowers discard to demote), so no output changes.

Skate 3's world fragment shader under SKATE_WORLD_SLOT_WATERFALL is one such loop: each trip
shades the subgroup's first material. On SM53 with perf14's NAK_DEBUG (xbar,sink,remat,xbrk) it
needed 64 GPRs, so 32 warps, against 56 GPRs and 36 warps without the loop, though about 55
values are live at once in either. With lsink it needs 56 and keeps 36 warps: 2134 instructions
and 15812 static cycles, against 2510 and 25119 without the loop. Every other shader in the
game's set (world, cutout, water, depth and Bevy's mesh) is within ten instructions of before,
in the same registers. No shader failed to compile or validate on the PC (nakhost, SM53, debug
build with assertions and NIR validation). Not yet run on hardware; off unless set. NVK's
shader cache key includes the NAK_DEBUG flags, so shaders built with and without it are cached
separately.

